ients we  really need to think about at the minute  and how we resource that  successive lockdowns are taking their  mental toll  not surprisingly the stress of isolation  compounded by anxiety over job losses  and the uncertainty over our futures is  to some extent  affecting us all but some people will  get support from family and friends  once the restrictions are eased  others might not be so resilient  dina will be looking out for these  people  using her own experiences to help others  struggling to cope it's been tough  um like with my own mental health  and obviously dealing with other  people's mental health  dina works for the mental health charity  mind  which in partnership with oxley's  provides community-based  support the uncertainty about what's  going to happen next  um is my job safe am i going to be  infected are my  people around me going to be infected  what's going to happen  like there's so much fear and that's  kind of played a part of my own  anxieties and how i'm going to  cope day to day dina's mental health  struggle began when she was just 13  years old  she's been getting much better but last  july went back on medication  this pandemic is affecting people with a  history of mental health problems  but also others who are experiencing  issues  for the first time we see people from  all walks of life  um there are people that have come  through to our services who have no  experience with mental health  difficulties it's all completely new to  them and like suddenly their whole world  has changed  and they're coming to terms with that as  well and plucking up the courage to be  able to ask for help  the first person emailed in to say um as  we're nearly a year into this pandemic  and whilst we have a vaccine and  infection numbers have started to fall  i'm finding life particularly hard at  the moment the pandemic has forced  mental health service providers to adapt  this therapy session has moved online  here people who were once servic